In this project, I was working as both a data engineer and machine learning engineer, which was an interesting contrast. I have always been more focused on the latter, as I felt challenged by the data cleaning stage. While a lot of the methods explored where concepts I was familiar with, I often forgot to check specific constraints such as having trouble uploading the data to my repository or encountering similar issues. Therefore, I quickly realized how despite having learned how to do something, it is always good to keep practicing in the long run to ensure that you don't forget it. 

It was interesting specifically using k-mean clustering for this project, as I had not used it in a long time. Specifically, I liked how we were allowed to use heuristics to interpret the customer segmentation portion of the project, as the different items that they purchased can be highlighted by code, but the interpretation as to why a specific group of customers specifically bought these products can vary drastically from person to person.  

Additionally, both PCA and Truncated SVD are topics that we learned recently in class, however I was amazed at how quickly this project made me see the real-world application and connection from dimensionality reduction to developing a pragmatic solution for the customer. I found very interesting the contrast between what we did last evaluating eigenvalues and eigenvectors by hand and automizing all of these processes with Python, I felt that doing both really helped me see a holistic approach of breaking down SVD and PCA and understanding the components first prior to then applying them to a real-world scenario such as this project.  

Being completely transparent, if I was able to do something differently, it would be to prepare myself prior to this project with GitHub. As a data analyst, I have used this tool previously extensively to publish some of my projects and was familiar with some typical components of a repository such as a "readme" file or a requirements file. However, I had forgotten how attempting to connect a Python virtual environment to both Google Drive (to process the data) and GitHub (to present the project) was an experience that was prone to encountering errors, therefore I felt that I did spent a good amount of time trying to troubleshoot these errors during the project. However, I am grateful for the experience because as I mentioned previously, I do feel it is important to continue to practice tools that are widely use in the machine learning and data analysis realm. 
